# The Engagement in the Sunday Milestones

Ruth Fairbanks reads the Sunday paper on the porch.

She has done it in the same order for fifty-one summers — front page, obituaries, Milestones, town news, the small local column at the back where a woman named Alice Peabody has been writing about who has come home from where since 1994. Ruth takes the sections apart with a small folding motion Charles taught her at the breakfast table in the summer of 1971, the summer they were first married, on the second floor of a saltbox on Washington Street in Marblehead they rented for eighty-five dollars a month from a widow named Mrs. Trefry, who liked Charles because he fixed her screen door on a Saturday morning without being asked.

Charles died in 2020.

The porch is quieter now. The paper is not.

## What page nine says

On the third Sunday in July, on page nine of the *Marblehead Reporter,* under the small unfussy heading *Milestones,* in nine-point Bodoni above a photograph the family provided of a young woman standing on the deck of a Herreshoff 12½ in Marblehead Harbor with a golden retriever named Griffin, is a single paragraph Ruth's daughter-in-law Kate wrote out by hand at the kitchen table on Tuesday afternoon and dropped off at the *Reporter's* office on School Street on Wednesday morning before her nine-thirty:

*Mr. and Mrs. Peter Fairbanks of Marblehead announce the engagement of their daughter Eleanor Hollis Fairbanks to Nathaniel Reynolds of Providence, son of Dr. and Mrs. James Reynolds of Bristol, Rhode Island. Miss Fairbanks is a 2019 graduate of a small Congregationalist college in the Pioneer Valley and is an associate at a Boston law firm. Mr. Reynolds is a 2018 graduate of Brown University and teaches high school English in Cambridge. A wedding is planned for Saturday, October 10, at Old North Church of Marblehead. The bride's late grandfather, Charles S. Fairbanks, was a longtime harbormaster of Marblehead.*

Ruth reads the paragraph three times.

She reads Eleanor twice — because Eleanor is her mother's name, and because it is what nobody but Ruth has called the girl since she was six weeks old, when Kate, who is a kind and careful daughter-in-law, gently began calling her Nell.

Ruth reads *Charles S. Fairbanks* once, and puts the paper down in her lap.

Her coffee, a Peet's blend Charles taught her to drink black on the porch in 1974, has gone cold.

## What the CRM has, on the same Sunday morning

Somewhere on the third floor of a converted red-brick building at the edge of the quad of a small Congregationalist women's college in the Pioneer Valley of western Massachusetts, on the same Sunday morning at nine-oh-seven, the record for Ruth H. Fairbanks, class of 1970, reads as follows.

*Address:* 12 Front Street, Marblehead, Massachusetts.
*Class:* 1970.
*Degree:* Bachelor of Arts, English literature.
*Spouse:* Charles S. Fairbanks (deceased 2020).
*Giving since:* 1972.
*Last gift:* November 4, 2023. $100. Annual fund.
*Status:* Lapsed since 2024.

There is no field on the record for *Eleanor Hollis Fairbanks, granddaughter, born June 1998, class of 2019.*

There is no field on the record for *Nathaniel Reynolds, Brown '18, teaches high school English in Cambridge.*

There is no field on the record for *the deck of a Herreshoff 12½ in Marblehead Harbor.*

There is no field on the record for *Old North Church of Marblehead, Saturday, October 10, three o'clock.*

There is no field on the record for the fact that Ruth's granddaughter — the girl who wrote her college essay in the fall of her senior year of high school about a hymnal her grandmother had kept on the piano bench in the parlor at 12 Front Street since the summer of 1974 — chose Ruth's own alma mater because Ruth had told her, one evening on the porch in the July of 2014, that a woman in her Restoration Comedy seminar named Miss Ida Colton had, in a single Wednesday-afternoon office hour in the fall of 1968, told Ruth she wrote well enough to teach.

The record does not know that Ruth, on the porch, is holding, in her cold coffee hand, a photograph of the girl she taught to identify a Herreshoff 12½ from an O'Day when the girl was five.

By September, the annual appeal from the Pioneer Valley will land in Ruth's mailbox at 12 Front Street. It will begin, *Dear Friend.* It will not mention Nell. It will not mention Charles. It will not mention the wedding — for which Ruth, in the last week of July, has already begun quietly thinking about the small, unfashionable, still-planned gift she and Charles had talked about the winter before he died, sitting side by side at the kitchen table with a legal pad, working out the numbers on the college by the light of the small lamp over the stove.

Ruth will read the first paragraph.

She will put the letter, still open, on the front hall table.

She will mean to write the check.

## What the paper does on Wednesday

Alice Peabody, seventy-three, writes the *Marblehead Reporter's* Milestones column and has since 1994. She keeps a manila file on the back corner of her desk on School Street with a scalloped label that reads *Engagements — 2026.* Inside the file, in Alice's small unhurried hand, are one hundred and forty index cards. On the top card for the third week of July, Alice has written:

*Eleanor Fairbanks / Nathaniel Reynolds. Old North, Oct 10. Bride is Peter & Kate Fairbanks's — Charlie & Ruthie's granddaughter. Charlie's granddaughter.*

Alice, who was two years behind Ruth Higgins at Marblehead High School, does not send the card to anyone in the Pioneer Valley.

She has never been asked to.

## What Rōmy does with page nine

Rōmy reads the small papers.

All of them. It reads the *Marblehead Reporter* and the *Salem News* and the *Beverly Citizen.* It reads the *Newburyport Daily News* and the *Ipswich Chronicle* and the *Cape Ann Beacon.* It reads the small *Milestones* column on page nine, the small society column at the back, the wedding announcement in the *Boston Globe* magazine that runs on the Sunday after Labor Day. It reads, in the plain unhurried way Miss Ida Colton read the small provincial theater notices in a 1968 issue of the *New Yorker* on the third floor of a Pioneer Valley library, every column-inch of every weekly the granddaughter of an alumna might appear in.

It cross-references — quietly, in the corner of the associate director of advancement's screen at eight-fifty on a Monday morning — every name against the CRM.

On the Monday morning after Alice's Sunday paper, Rōmy puts, at the top of the associate director's list, a small quiet note:

*Ruth H. Fairbanks, class of 1970 (English literature, thesis with Ida Colton on Aphra Behn, June 1970), longtime donor from 1972 to 2023, widow of Charles S. Fairbanks (longtime harbormaster of Marblehead, deceased 2020). Her granddaughter Eleanor Hollis Fairbanks, class of 2019 — same major, honors in Restoration Comedy — announced her engagement in the Marblehead Reporter on Sunday to Nathaniel Reynolds, Brown '18. Wedding at Old North Church of Marblehead, Saturday, October 10. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand, to 12 Front Street, Marblehead — congratulating Ruth by name on Nell (not Eleanor — Kate has been calling her Nell since she was six weeks old, and Ruth is the only one in the family who still writes Eleanor on the birthday envelope), mentioning Charles if it isn't a bother, and — only if it isn't — asking whether Ruth would sit with you for a coffee at the small tea room on Washington Street the next time you are on the North Shore. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

The card goes out on Tuesday.

Ruth reads it on Thursday, on the porch, with her second cup of Peet's.

She writes back on Friday, in the small round hand she has been writing thank-you notes in since Miss Perley's fourth-grade classroom at Bell School in the fall of 1956.

By September the coffee is on the calendar. By October the associate director sits in the fourth row on the left at Old North Church of Marblehead, in a plain navy dress and low heels, and cries in the good undramatic way at the part of the ceremony when the minister mentions Charles. By December Ruth is on the phone with the associate director's boss, a woman named Margaret whose own daughter went to the same Pioneer Valley college in 2011, working out the numbers on the small unfashionable gift Ruth and Charles had talked about the winter before he died — this time in Ruth's small round hand, on the same yellow legal pad Charles had used, on the kitchen table at 12 Front Street, at 7:42 on a Sunday evening in the second week of Advent.

In the memo line of the check that arrives in the Pioneer Valley on the Tuesday before Christmas, in blue ink, in twenty characters:

*In memory of Charles.*

Page nine was never the point. The Herreshoff was never the point. The wedding was never the point.

The *reading* was the point.

## A small assignment, with love ♡

This week, before anything else, do one small unfashionable thing.

Go to your CRM. Pull the ten lapsed alumnae in your file whose grandchildren are, by the plain arithmetic of a birthdate you can guess within a decade, of an age to be engaged this summer. If you cannot remember which ten, pick the ten whose last gift was under two hundred dollars, whose loyalty on your file goes back forty summers, and whose spouse's obituary you can, in the small back room of your office, still remember reading.

Open a new tab. Type the name of the small weekly in each of the ten hometowns. If the paper has a website — and most of them, in Massachusetts and Maine and North Carolina and Ohio, still do — click on *Milestones,* or *Engagements,* or *Community.* If the paper does not have a website, call the town clerk's office and ask, in your kindest voice, whether the secretary would mail you the last four Sundays. She will. She has been waiting for a reason to.

Read page nine.

If a granddaughter's name — or a grandson's — is set in it, take a plain cream card from the top drawer of the credenza.

Do not put a case for support in it.

Do not put a QR code in it.

Do not sign it with a title.

Write three sentences, in blue ink, in your own hand. Say the alumna's name once. Say the small specific thing the paper printed — the girl's name, the boat in the harbor, the church at three o'clock in October. Say, in the plain language of a person who was paying attention, that you saw her on page nine.

Walk it, on your lunch break, to the post office on your corner.

Old North Church of Marblehead has been standing since 1824.

Alice Peabody has been writing *Milestones* on School Street since 1994.

The third Sunday in July has been reserved, in Ruth Fairbanks's fifty-one-summer order of reading, for page nine.

Turn to it. ♡
